;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/append-reverse-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "append-reverse!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(append-reverse! rev-head tail) -> list"
  :summary "The elements of rev-head in reverse order followed by tail, built by turning rev-head's own cells around. rev-head is destroyed."
  :explanation "append-reverse! is the accumulator-flushing verb. The standard way to build a list is to cons onto an accumulator, which leaves it backwards, and then reverse it -- and if there is something to attach afterwards, append-reverse does both steps in one pass. The destructive form does it without allocating: it walks rev-head reversing each cdr in place, and the last cell it turns around is made to point at tail. The suite gives the value at list.sld:358. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. Note the asymmetry, because it is the useful part: rev-head is destroyed and tail is NOT. tail is simply the thing the reversed cells end up pointing at, so passing a shared list as tail is safe, while passing one as rev-head is not. That asymmetry is what makes the verb usable in a loop whose accumulator is private and whose result tail is not."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(display (append-reverse! (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5 6)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; rev-head is consumed; tail is embedded untouched.\n(define acc (list 3 2 1))\n(define tail (list 4 5))\n(define out (append-reverse! acc tail))\n(display (list 'out out 'tail-embedded (eq? (cdddr out) tail)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; the accumulator flush: build backwards, then flush onto what follows.\n(define (collect-then-flush items rest)\n  (let loop ((xs items) (acc (list)))\n    (if (null? xs)\n        (append-reverse! acc rest)\n        (loop (cdr xs) (cons (car xs) acc)))))\n(display (collect-then-flush (list 1 2 3) (list 'end)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("rev-head is destroyed; tail is not. list.sld:358 gives the value, and the asymmetry follows from reversing rev-head's cells in place."
             "tail is embedded, not copied, so later mutation of tail shows through the result."
             "tail need not be a proper list -- whatever it is becomes the terminator, exactly as in append-reverse."
             "Passing the same list as both arguments builds a cycle.")
  :drawbacks ("The asymmetry between the two arguments is invisible at the call site and easy to get backwards."
             "It only pays off when rev-head is a private accumulator, which is a narrower situation than it appears."
             "Reversing in place makes rev-head unusable even for error reporting after the call.")
  :usecases ("Flushing a cons-built accumulator onto an existing tail at the end of a loop."
             "Building a list front-to-back in linear time with no intermediate reversal allocation."
             "The last step of a hand-written map or filter written with an accumulator.")
  :related ("append-reverse"
             "reverse!"
             "append!"
             "reverse"
             "fold")
  :learn (:concept "Accumulator flushing, and reading argument asymmetry"
          :prerequisites ("reverse" "append-reverse" "cons" "fold")
          :progression "Write a loop that conses onto an accumulator and reverses at the end. Then replace the last two steps with append-reverse and see the pass disappear.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:358; signature derived from the append-reverse record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(display (append-reverse! (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 3 2 1))\n(define b (list 3 2 1))\n(display (equal? (append-reverse a (list 4)) (append-reverse! b (list 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define tail (list 4 5))\n(define out (append-reverse! (list 3 2 1) tail))\n(display (eq? (cdddr out) tail))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has finished a cons-accumulator loop and needs the result in order, attached to an existing tail. Candidates: (append (reverse acc) tail) (two passes, two allocations), (append-reverse acc tail) (one pass, one allocation, acc survives), or append-reverse! (one pass, no allocation, acc destroyed). Because acc is private to the loop by construction, this is one of the few places where the destructive choice is genuinely uncontroversial."
     :explanation "eng: the accumulator comes back the right way round and joined to the tail. math: append-reverse!(h, t) = append(reverse(h), t) for every proper h. cs: tail is embedded rather than copied, which is what makes the operation allocation-free and what makes a shared tail still safe."
     :meta "This is the case that justifies the whole linear-update family: a value with exactly one owner, produced and consumed inside one function.")
    (:eng "The clean case for destruction is a value that was born and dies inside one function."
     :math "append-reverse fuses a reversal and a concatenation into one traversal."
     :cs "Argument asymmetry is a design tool: destroy the private one, share the public one.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Turning a backwards-built pile the right way round and attaching it to what follows."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(display (append-reverse! (list 3 2 1) (list 4)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a stack of plates emptied onto a shelf" :code "(define stack (list 'top 'middle 'bottom))\n(define shelf (list 'existing))\n(display (append-reverse! stack shelf))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a shunting siding cleared onto the main line" :code "(define siding (list 'w3 'w2 'w1))\n(define main (list 'm1))\n(display (append-reverse! siding main))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the cells of rev-head are turned around and the last points at tail. Emptying a stack of plates onto a shelf takes them off in the opposite order to the one they went on, which is the reversal, and the shelf is not disturbed. Clearing a shunting siding onto the main line is the same move: the siding empties, the main line grows, and the wagons come out reversed. All three share a last-in-first-out store discharging into a first-in-first-out one."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a stack empties into a queue -- the reversal is not an extra step, it is what the discharge IS.")
    (:eng "The reversal is free because it is the discharge order, not a separate operation."
     :math "A stack read out is a reversed sequence; that is the definition, not a consequence."
     :cs "This is why accumulator-then-reverse is the standard list-building idiom rather than a workaround.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define out (append-reverse! (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5)))\n(display (list 'out out 'len (length out)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (law? h t)\n  (let ((h2 (list-copy h)))\n    (equal? (append-reverse! h2 t) (append (reverse h) t))))\n(display (list (law? (list 3 2 1) (list 4)) (law? (list) (list 4)) (law? (list 1) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define t (list 4 5))\n(define out (append-reverse! (list 3 2 1) t))\n(set-car! t 99)\n(display (list 'tail-mutation-visible out))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a proper list rev-head and any object tail, append-reverse! returns a value equal? to (append (reverse rev-head) tail). rev-head's cells are re-used to build the result; tail is embedded unchanged and is not copied."
     :invariant "forall proper h, any t. equal?(append-reverse!(h, t), append(reverse(h), t)) and t is a suffix of the result by identity, not by copy"
     :meta "The pattern is fused reverse-and-append. What would falsify the claim: disagreement with the two-step form, or a copied tail -- and the empty-rev-head case, where the result must be tail itself, is the boundary that catches implementations that always allocate.")
    (:eng "The empty-accumulator case must return the tail unchanged; that is where an always-allocating implementation shows itself."
     :math "The law append-reverse(h,t) = append(reverse(h),t) is the specification; the fusion is the optimisation."
     :cs "Testing tail identity with a mutation is the only way to see the difference from the pure form.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (flush! acc rest) (append-reverse! acc rest))\n(display (flush! (list 3 2 1) (list 4)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (my-map f xs)\n  (let loop ((rest xs) (acc (list)))\n    (if (null? rest)\n        (append-reverse! acc (list))\n        (loop (cdr rest) (cons (f (car rest)) acc)))))\n(display (my-map (lambda (n) (* n n)) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (my-reverse xs) (append-reverse xs (list)))\n(display (my-reverse (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "append-reverse! turns a backwards accumulator round in place and attaches it to a tail."
     :emergent "eng: the flush step, named. math: a hand-written map falls out of it -- accumulate forwards, flush at the end -- and this is how map is actually implemented in most Schemes. cs: reverse itself is append-reverse with an empty tail, which is worth noticing because it means reverse! and append-reverse! are one function with a default argument."
     :meta "The family is accumulator discipline. What emerges is that reverse, map, filter and append-reverse are all the same loop with different bodies, and the flush is the only part that varies.")
    (:eng "Once you see the flush, every list-building loop looks the same, and that is the point."
     :math "reverse is the special case with an empty tail; the general verb subsumes it."
     :cs "This is why a Scheme implementation can define reverse!, map and filter over one internal helper.")
  )
)
